  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What makes a living thing a plant?",
      "a": "Plants are many-celled, have cell walls made of cellulose and chloroplasts, and make their own food by photosynthesis.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about plants"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What makes a living thing an animal?",
      "a": "Animals are many-celled, have no cell walls or chloroplasts, eat other living things for food and can usually move from place to place.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about animals"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Are mushrooms plants?",
      "a": "No. Mushrooms are fungi. Fungi have no chloroplasts, so they cannot make food; they feed by releasing enzymes onto dead material and soaking up the result. Their cell walls are made of chitin, not cellulose.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about fungi"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the Monera kingdom?",
      "a": "Monera are the prokaryotes: single cells with no nucleus, such as bacteria. Their DNA floats loose in the cytoplasm. Today many scientists split them into two domains, Bacteria and Archaea.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about bacteria"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are protists?",
      "a": "Protists are mostly single-celled living things that do have a nucleus, such as amoeba, paramecium and many algae. The protist that causes malaria, Plasmodium, belongs here too.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about protists"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The five-kingdom system was proposed by Robert Whittaker in 1969.",
    "Fungal cell walls are made of chitin, while plant cell walls are made of cellulose.",
    "Marine phytoplankton produce roughly half of the oxygen made on Earth each year."
  ],
